<p>A federal jury in San Francisco found Elon Musk liable in a class-action securities case over his 2022 purchase of Twitter, concluding that he misled shareholders with statements he made while trying to renegotiate or exit the $44 billion takeover agreement. Reports say jurors found that two tweets from May 2022 contained false statements, and damages could reach roughly $2.5 billion to $2.6 billion, though a final payout has not yet been set. The ruling is a notable test of shareholder accountability in a case centered on whether public posts by a high-profile executive can unlawfully move markets. The dispute grew out of Musk’s claims that Twitter underreported fake and spam accounts. Shareholders said those comments pushed down the share price while he was seeking leverage over the deal. Musk ultimately completed the acquisition in October 2022 and later renamed the platform X.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cRDCnRt8xbl7VsjDfbaFtqzdWzR02LxdJLkiZ3CwJnCtAYUZ9wuJPmQLjreaScbHB9mjAo5n-sDY7oq6xk6CJMoAT_pVhGR0udQUNkbC4M4O' alt='Elon Musk attends the opening ceremony of the new Tesla Gigafactory for electric cars in Gruenheide, Germany on March 22, 2022.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Appeal planned: Musk’s lawyers at Quinn Emanuel Urquhart &amp; Sullivan called the verdict “a bump in the road” and said they expect to seek vindication on appeal.</li><li>Trial timing: The civil trial began on March 2, 2026, and jurors started deliberating Tuesday before reaching a verdict Friday.</li><li>Liability scope: Jurors reviewed three challenged statements and found Musk liable for two of them, according to reporting on the verdict.</li><li>Prior litigation: The case adds to Musk’s long history of courtroom fights with shareholders, including his successful defenses in a 2023 Tesla “funding secured” trial and separate Delaware litigation over his Tesla pay package.</li></ul><blockquote>Musk’s status as the world’s richest man is not a free pass - Francis Bottini</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Twitter shareholders: Shareholders argued that Musk falsely claimed Twitter understated bots and spam accounts, and said he used those statements to pressure the company’s stock lower during the takeover fight. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3347400/elon-musk-misled-twitter-shareholders-us-jury-finds'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Musk’s legal team: His lawyers called the verdict a temporary setback and signaled they would appeal rather than accept the jury’s findings. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1984219/elon-musk-found-liable-to-twitter-shareholders-in-fraud-lawsuit-over-44-billion-takeover'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Plaintiffs’ counsel: Lawyer Francis Bottini framed the case as a warning that executives who can move markets through social media still face consequences if investors are harmed. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1984219/elon-musk-found-liable-to-twitter-shareholders-in-fraud-lawsuit-over-44-billion-takeover'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3347400/elon-musk-misled-twitter-shareholders-us-jury-finds'>Elon Musk misled Twitter shareholders, US jury finds</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/21/us-jury-finds-elon-musk-misled-twitter-shareholders_6751658_4.html'>US jury finds Elon Musk misled Twitter shareholders</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/88161509-efe4-4b7b-8465-d66645b445d0'>Musk defrauded Twitter investors during buyout, jury finds</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c62j3yl842eo'>Elon Musk misled Twitter investors, jury finds</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/21/us-jury-finds-elon-musk-misled-investors-during-twitter-purchase'>US jury finds Elon Musk misled investors during Twitter purchase</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://w